  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">There are two national trade union centres in in Mozambique, Organiza&#xe7;&#xe3;o dos Trabalhadores de Mozambiquen (OTM_-CS) and Confedera&#xe7;&#xe3;o Nacional dos Sindicatos Independentes e Livres de Mo&#xe7;ambique (CONSLIMO). However, the union are faced with low capacity in understanding the role of MNEs and their impact on local job creation, working conditions, skills development and industrial relations in Mozambique and be able to influence policy within the framework of the MNE Declaration including Labour Law reforms from an international labour standards (ILS) positioning workers&#x2019; organisation to support and engage in the implementation of the public service reforms; minimum wage setting; mechanism for social dialogue; and education and training of workers in respect to rights as provided for by the labour laws. Therefore, the ILO through this Country Programme Outcome are focusing on strengthening the institutional, technical, representational and policy impact capacities of workers&#x2019; organizations. The strategy interventions include selecting key sectors (Construction) and promote labour rights by targeting key systemic constraints in the national construction sectors, which keep most construction workers locked in low wage, unsafe, and precarious work, often in informal employment.
The CPO is also focusing on improving the governance of occupational safety and health through new policies, programmes or strengthened institutions. As a starting point the this Country Programme Outcome (CPO) will facilitate the undertaking of a national Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) profile which is a situation analysis summarizing the features, strengths and weaknesses of the national OSH system and the progress made towards the achievement of a safe and healthy working environment, as defined and with the content identified by Section IV of the Promotional Framework for Occupational Safety and Health Recommendation, 2006 (No. 197).
The beneficiaries of this intervention include worker</narrative>
  </description>
